# Chapter 8 Exercises

## 8-1 Construct a query that counts the number of rows in the payment table
### Solution (SQL Query)
mysql> SELECT count(*)
    -> FROM payment;
+----------+
| count(*) |
+----------+
|    16044 |
+----------+
1 row in set (0.03 sec)

## 8-2 Modify your query from Exercise 8-1 to count the number of payments made by each
customer. Show the customer ID and the total amount paid for each customer
### Solution (SQL Query)
mysql> SELECT customer_id, count(*) num_of_payments, sum(amount) amount_paid
    -> FROM payment
    -> GROUP BY customer_id;
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
| customer_id | num_of_payments | amount_paid |
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
|           1 |              32 |      118.68 |
|           2 |              27 |      128.73 |
|           3 |              26 |      135.74 |
...
|         598 |              22 |       83.78 |
|         599 |              19 |       83.81 |
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
599 rows in set (0.04 sec)

## 8-3 Modify your query from Exercise 8-2 to include only those customers who have
made at least 40 payments
### Solution (SQL Query)
mysql> SELECT customer_id, count(*) num_of_payments, sum(amount) amount_paid
    -> FROM payment
    -> GROUP BY customer_id
    -> HAVING count(*) >= 40;
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
| customer_id | num_of_payments | amount_paid |
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
|          75 |              41 |      155.59 |
|         144 |              42 |      195.58 |
|         148 |              46 |      216.54 |
|         197 |              40 |      154.60 |
|         236 |              42 |      175.58 |
|         469 |              40 |      177.60 |
|         526 |              45 |      221.55 |
+-------------+-----------------+-------------+
7 rows in set (0.11 sec)
